Date: Tue, 16 Mar 93 01:39:33 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: The Prolific Society

I take it we're supposed to each think up a PPL Society?

For _my_ society (Charter Memberships now available), I am proposing the
"Prolific Society."

Any member of The Prolific Society may challenge another member to _write
longer posts_. He says to the challengee: "Sir (or Madam), I find your
posts of inadequate length." The challengee must then write more total
words in the next week than the challenger.


....But seriously:

Robin's idea is interesting, but I see several problems:

* The accounting could be a problem. I don't expect most of us will carry
around records of how many postings, how many challenges (and by whom?),
etc. This is solvable, but adds to the hassles of being on the Net and on
mailing lists.

* Precisely what problem does this solve? Rudeness? IMHO, we're remarkably
polite (Pandit may not think so, be we listened to his religious rants for
many months before finally calling a spade a spade.) In fact, I happen to
think we're too polite, too accepting.

Consider a controversial poster--Pandit or Perry or me (take your pick).
The odds are quite high that a controversial posting will anger at least
one reader enough to take Pandit or Perry or me out of the posting business
for a week or a month. A small coterie of anti-Tim folks (just an example)
may even "rotate coverage" so that I'm off the list indefinitely. 

* Put differently, various posters will value posting privileges
differently. For example, I may consider being able to post to be very
valuable, while a casual member of the list (busy with other things, for
example) may place much less value on posting. I will not be willing to
lose my posting privileges casually, while others might.

(Tom Morrow dismissed this point earlier by asserting that perhaps others
would in fact value posting and reading privileges nearly as much as active
posters do:

"I don't think the cost is minimal, as Joe loses *all* access to the list. 
If he has been hanging around as a lurker, he must value reading others'
posts.  Being knocked off for a week will cause him to lose this benefit. 
And he must think it is of considerable value, for reading this list takes
up quite a bit of time!"

I don't think there's much evidence to support this assertion.)

* Many who are kicked out will elect to simply not get back into the swing
of things.

* Unlike "real" shootings in the Wild West (which have likely been
exagerated over the years, by the way), zapping another poster is too
painless, too easy. Those going away for a vacation, for example, my choose
to zap a pesky poster--knowing it won't really hurt their own posting
careers. And those who want to just take a break may take someone with
them.

(This gets back to the imprecise "valuation" of posting privileges.)


Thanks, but no thanks.

Granted, all of these societies are voluntary. I'll be watching with
interest. An interesting experiment.

-Tim

P.S. to Robin. Suppose I join the Polite Society and, two weeks from now,
"shoot" you. We're both off the list for, say, a week. After a week, I
shoot you again. And again. And so on.

Perhaps I'm doing this to make some kind of weird point (as I did with my
various prefixes!) or perhaps I just want to get some work done and don't
really mind being off the list for a while. In any case, we're both off the
list for as long as I keep it up.

Comments?
 
