This is no big deal; it is simply that sloppy reasoning irks me.  Tim May
writes:

>* Put differently, various posters will value posting privileges
>differently. For example, I may consider being able to post to be very
>valuable, while a casual member of the list (busy with other things, for
>example) may place much less value on posting. I will not be willing to
>lose my posting privileges casually, while others might.
>
>(Tom Morrow dismissed this point earlier by asserting that perhaps others
>would in fact value posting and reading privileges nearly as much as active
>posters do:

No.  Tim wrote only of *posting* privileges.  I was speaking of *posting
and reading* privileges.  I thus did not "dismiss [Tim's] point."  In fact,
I emphasized the loss of reading privileges precisely because I do not
think that lurkers will find the threat of losing mere posting privileges
much of a threat.  

I thus agree with Tim that Robin's proposal will fail to give lurkers much
of a disincentive.  But I disagree that this says much about my comments;
if anything, it again emphasizes the importance of affecting the rights to
both post and read.

>"I don't think the cost is minimal, as Joe loses *all* access to the list. 
>If he has been hanging around as a lurker, he must value reading others'
>posts.  Being knocked off for a week will cause him to lose this benefit. 
>And he must think it is of considerable value, for reading this list takes
>up quite a bit of time!"
>
>I don't think there's much evidence to support this assertion.)

The proof for it is quite evident:  reading this list takes time.  Time is
(to a greater or lesser extent for differently situated people) money. 
Reading the list also ties up hardware.  And some people incur a fee for
using a private email service.

The question is whether there is any evidence to disprove my assertion.  Do
you suppose you can remove current subscribers without them objecting?  Of
course not.  If they wanted to leave they could do so themselves with great
ease (though apparently this may require sending a message to the list,
rather than just to Extropians-request).  The fact that they continue to
subscribe shows that they ascribe a positive net benefit to doing so.

