Date: Tue, 16 Mar 93 08:36:17 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: ECON/LAW: Tragedy of the commons

Perhaps it is relevant to cite the reason for the existence of
copyrights and patents, and to then discuss whether the reasoning
is correct:

"The Congress shall have power. . . 8. To promote the progress of
science and useful arts by securing for limited times to authors
and inventors the exclusive rights to their respective writings and
discoveries; "  [US Constitution, Article I, Section 8]

So, the declared purpose of patents and copyrights is to promote
the progress of science and technology [useful arts].  Note the
qualification on 'arts'.  The description was not merely 'science
and the arts', but 'science and the useful arts'.  It would seem
the intent was to promote that which was useful to society, and
not necessarily things like literature.

The method chosen to promote progress was to (a) encourage the
exploration of 'idea space' by granting rights to the first
explorer of an idea (invention or writing), but then (b) making
that right temporary, so as to eventually put that idea in the
public domain, and available to all to use.

We might think of a continuum ranging from no protection of ideas,
through limited time ownership, to perpetual ownership of an idea,
i.e. a copyright or patent never expires.  Then a valid question
would be where on the continuum do we get the most progress in
science and technology?

In the case of patents, I currently think that 17 years is too
short for maximum incentives.  There are a lot of ideas that I 
have been told to put aside at work because thay are not near
term enough to be exploited (like stuff relating to lunar bases).
Whether the decision was right or wrong in the particular case
is not important.  In many cases young engineers get ideas, but
they can't implement them until late in their careers, when they
have risen to a level of authority in an organization.  So the
characteristic time may be 20-40 years for a new idea.  The
patent protection should be of a comparable tijme span.  In the
case of new drugs, the approval process of a new drug takes so 
long that most of the patent time has expired before the drug
company can make any money off it.  In this case also, the
patent interval should be somewhat longer.  My own opinion is
a 30 year duration is about right.

In the case of copyright, which was recently extended to life of
author + 50 years, I see no reason for the period to be so long.
The downstream royalties from a book, movie, or other data source
are of negligible value 50-100 years from now.

Dani Eder

