Subject: Econ Drug War: Clinton Ratchets back the War On Drugs?

This was written by my friend Mike Hershdorfer, a lawyer friend in DC, and
it is the tightest lump of anti-war on drugs data I've ever run accross.

He's a Clinton fan, too, so try to ignore that. Still, I wonder if a
'public health policy' for drug use, even though misguided from the
libertarian point of view, might be less 'coercive' in the long run
(cost less/put fewer people in jail) and as such might be preferable? 

Given that the real solution, the end of prohibition, is socioculturally
still about a generation to two away?

Mike Hershdorfer writes:

I read a good article on the drug war in my ABA
Journal.  Some quickie  facts:

There are more people in federal prisons for drug offenses today than the 
total for all offenses in 1980.  Former Drug Czar  William Bennett insisted 
that drug abuse take precedence over poverty, hunger, AIDS and alcoholism 
at the Dept of Health and Human Services.  This even though Bennett opposed 
approaching drug issues from a public health perspective.  James Gray, a 
conservative Republican superior court judge advocates legaization of 
drugs.  He and several of his collegues realize that drug cases overwhelm 
the justice system.  FBI figures reveal that the huge outlay of government 
spending and new draconian laws have not affected violent or property crime 
rates.  Illegal drug consumption levels remain steady as well.  Medical 
researchers at Emory University Medical School and at the National 
Association of Prenatal Research and Education challenge the view that 
"crack babies" are permanently debilitated because of their mother's 
cocaine use.  Their studies show that harm from cocaine exposure is 
temporary.  But poverty, malnutrition, violence and lack of prenatal care 
cause permanent harm.  Studies reveal that the often asserted link between 
drug use and violent crime is a myth.  In 1988, of 218 New York City 
homicides recorded as "drug related" 5 were linked to crack users, but 21 
to alcohol users.  Regarding crack homicides, arguably  the illegal status 
of the susbstance, rather than any inherent propery, created the motive.  
Turf wars and the need to steal to pay artificially high prices stem from 
drug laws rather than the drugs themselves.  Cocaine is not instantly 
addictive--despite drug war  hype.  According to the GAO, 6 million used 
Cocaine in 1990, 10% used it weekly, and only 5.4% used it daily. 

A dozen recent Supreme Court cases facilitated intrusive drug 
investigations, permitted pretrial detention, and denied equal protection 
under the law from those accused of drug offenses.  Federal judges approved 
100% of wire-tap requests--and federal wire tapping was 400% more frequent 
in 1990 than in 1980.  Courts granted leniency to people who abuse drug 
users such as parents who chained their daughter to a radiator, and a 
mother who killed her daughter because the daughters used drugs.  
Prosecuters charged 167  women with crimes from drug trafficing to homocide 
for using illegal drugs while pregnant--even though there aren't any 
applicable laws.  Many women are in jail or on probation for non-existant 
crimes.

Fortunately, the old guard is gone.  Former top Bush officials relied on 
rhetoric, not on research  to support the often repeated canard that drug 
use increases violent crime.  John Walters, who directed drug enforcement 
efforts in the Office of National Drug Control Policy admitted, "If you 
want mass studies, there aren't any."  He added, "It's a moral question" 
and "the question of right and wrong is crucial."  Former Attorney General 
Willaim Barr claimed building more prisons "is the morally right thing to 
do."  ONDCP "Czar" Bennett argued that drug use "makes a mockery of 
virtue."  Bennett even suggested beheading drug dealers while on CNN's 
Larry King Live show.   Reagan and Bush increased drug war spending from 
$1.5B in 1980 to  $13B in '90--2/3 for law enforcement.  States and 
localities paid an additional $18B  on drug control--80% for enforcement.  
Bill Clinton told the ABA Journal he believes "a large part of the 
substance-abuse problem can be best dealt with as a public health and 
education issue."  Clinton quickly cut 121 of 146 staff positions from the 
"drug czar's" office.  Deeper cuts to drug enforcement funding would 
further Clinton's deficit reduction efforts.  The President seems poised to 
push a health and education approach to the drug abuse problem, rejecting 
the punitive and sanctimonious approaches of the recent past.  Two 
Presidents led us into the War on Drugs.  Hopefully this President will 
lead us out again.

