*Discussing* the experience of an eclipse (which was one of the most 
astonishing experiences I ever had) is a pale shadow of the actual 
experience.  I have experienced social "raptures" in this emotional 
range--the one which comes most vividly to mind is the after dinner 
discussion which ran late into the night at the '75 Conference on 
Space Manufacturing where Dr. O'Neill, my ex wife Carolyn, Eric 
Drexler, and a number of others passionately promoted taking the idea 
of space colonies to the public and making a solid effort to garner 
enough support to actually bring them into existence.  (Didn't work, 
but useful things came out of that effort, such as Eric Drexler's many 
contacts and experience which has served him well in promoting 
nanotech.) 

Exciting ideas, sex, some drugs, music, large gatherings, and "peak" 
experiences both good and bad "imprint" on our nervous systems, often 
providing motivation (or at least vivid memories and stories to tell) 
for years to follow.  Religious organizations make an (often 
conscious) effort to produce this effect.  Even if you have *no* 
intellectual belief in a religion, attending a service will almost 
always kick off strong emotional responses.  I believe people have a 
*need* for these kinds of experiences.  Without (a moderate amount of) 
them, life is unsatisfying.  (It is, of course, all to easy to get too 
much, as I imagine the Branch Davidians, and the folks in Bosnia would 
likely attest.) 

The problem is:  how can us folks who just can't take seriously the 
religions we were raised in get our fix of religious ecstasy?  My take 
of the neo pagan way is to revive a dead tradition which has given 
people their thrills before, but today does not ask you to believe 
three impossible things before breakfast.  The nice thing about neo 
pagans is the lack of "official dogma"--at least yet.

To some unknown (but certainly small) degree *I* may be responsible 
for the neo pagan revival.  My friends in the last two years of high 
school got into using runes as a "secret" language to leave messages 
on the blackboards.  We studied Norse myths a little and adopted 
"club names" after various Norse gods or other mythological/historical 
characters --in much the same spirit as extropians have been doing.  I 
still use mine occasionally as a pen name.  In 1960 I started a 
tradition of tongue- in-cheek declaring to be "Druid" in the 
"religion" box when registering for classes at the University of 
Arizona.  It was actually quite adaptive.  NOBODY would argue religion 
with a self-declared pagan, they had no idea of what arguments to 
use, while putting down Atheist was like pinning a "kick me" sign on 
your shirt. 

I think most neo pagans enjoy themselves, rituals and all, while not 
taking it very seriously.  But memes of this class can *easily* get 
out of hand.  Several "joke" organizations such as the Flat Earth 
Society and the Society to Clothe animals grew into serious beliefs 
for a subset of members who "couldn't take a joke."  Over ten years 
after I founded the "Druid Student Center" I ran into somebody who was 
deadly serious about being a Druid.  Gave me the willies. 

I might add, that for all the effort put into making it nearly 
impossible to take them seriously, I expect some bozos will try to 
turn the Campus Crusade for Chthula (sp?), Discordians and SubGenius 
(TM) into serious religions. I was somewhat amused to hear that there 
are enough self declared neo pagans in the army for them to rate a 
certain number of *chaplains*. I have no idea of how they manage to 
fill the slots, but I suppose I could ask over in alt.pagan.  I wonder 
if one of them could apply for the positions of House or Senate 
chaplain?  :)  Keith Henson 

PS to Tim May.  You should include the last few pages of *Hackers 
Dictionary* in your evaluation, but don't forget Eric is almost as 
good at leg pulling as you are.  :)  KH

