Subject: PHIL/CHALLENGE: The hunger for transcendent experience

Keith made some good points about the human hunger for "peak experiences"
and the role that has in making religious memes attractive.  I'd say his
is a reasonable outsider's view of neopaganism; it *is* an important part
of neopaganism's value that it fulfills that hunger without requiring one
to dump on the scientific worldview.

That isn't the whole story, however.

First: many neopagans have had their first mystical experiences before
becoming neopagans.  For them, part of neopaganism's value is that it
supplies a context and vocabulary for these experiences that makes them
easier to use, cope with, and assimilate.

Second: mystically altered consciousness isn't just a "legal high".  It
has important functions which are poorly (if at all) understood in the
mainstream culture.  Most importantly, it's a way of inducing "imprint
vulnerability" so that one's aspects (life scripts, personae, sub-
personalities) can be reprogrammed.

Abuse this and you get David Koresh's hold over the Branch Davidians.
But it has many positive uses; for example, in curing or alleviating
the 85% of disease that even conventional MDs concede is psychosomatic
or stress-diasthetic.  Works even better on stuff that's pure-functional;
for example, I once cured a woman of writer's block with a spell (no joke).
